questions.answer:
**Transient** gives you a new instance every time it is resolved, including twice within the same request. **Scoped** gives you one instance per scope, and ASP.NET Core creates a scope per request, so it means one per request. **Singleton** gives you one instance for the lifetime of the application, created from the root provider.

Pick by state: stateless helpers can be transient, anything holding per-request state (a `DbContext`, a unit of work, the current user's context) must be scoped, and anything expensive and genuinely shared (a cache, a client factory, an in-memory index) is a singleton — and therefore must be thread-safe, because many requests will be inside it at once.

A **captive dependency** is a longer-lived service holding a reference to a shorter-lived one. A singleton that injects a scoped `DbContext` captures the very first request's instance and keeps it forever: after that request ends the context is disposed, and every later use either throws `ObjectDisposedException` or serves another request's tracked entities. The shorter lifetime has been *captured* by the longer one and effectively promoted to singleton.

ASP.NET Core catches most of this for you — scope validation is on by default in the **Development** environment, so `builder.Build()` throws "Cannot consume scoped service from singleton". It is off in Production. Turn it on everywhere:

```csharp
builder.Host.UseDefaultServiceProvider(o =>
{
    o.ValidateScopes = true;
    o.ValidateOnBuild = true;
});
```

Java engineers: Spring's default singleton beans get away with injecting request-scoped ones because Spring injects a *proxy*. .NET injects the real instance, so the bug is real.
